WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:12.629 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  rah  this primal  guttural chant explodes to the throats  of 250 boys and girls signaling the  start of the camp day  but i have no idea what's going on  i've arrived at camp a week late and i  feel lost  can you imagine that scared  seven-year-old trembling in the back row  that's me  terrified i won't belong  scared i won't make friends  that was 20 years ago and i was about to  learn the lesson i want to share with  you all now  the profound impact an invested adult  can have on a child's life  for me that adult was tarzan tim  back in the summer of 2002 tarzan tim  was the coolest guy around  he wore ray-bans and a visor before the  retro look was in  he was bigger faster and stronger than  everyone at camp  and tarzan tim set out to be my friend  me  that skinny seven-year-old with more  anxiety in his gut than he could handle  himself i remember the way that tarzan  tim made me feel  feel like i belonged  he would say things like whoa mario sure  knows how to make a mud ball you all  should be asking him how he does it  or wow mario that's a fantastic dream  catcher we'll have to hang it in our  secret fort  these small moments of investment turned  a terrifying experience into a summer of  social and emotional growth  tarzan tim invested in me  and it transformed my life  summer camp has the ability to inspire  transformative change in young people's  lives  full stop the national academy of  sciences highlights summer camp benefits  on child safety  social emotional well-being  physical and mental health and academic  learning  an ongoing longitudinal study by  psychologist david yeager indicates that  a mere one week at camp can increase  college attendance rates by 18  and that's one week  imagine the power of a full summer  so the stats tell us we do good by our  children when we send them to camp where  the power of an invested adult works  wonders  but that's not the only thing i'm here  to talk about with you today  this  this picture of me and tarzan tim back  in that fabled summer of 2002.  beyond the smiles and the mud stained  shirts  what stands out to me  the general lack of ethnic diversity  my memory may be fuzzy but i'm willing  to bet that tim was the only this summer  at camp  i can say across my 16 years at camp  tim was the only black counselor in my  group  the american camp association states  that 70 of summer campers are white  americans and this under-represents the  true figure at private for-profit  sleep-away camps across the entire  camping landscape only five percent of  camps cater to a majority impoverished  population  it paid me to think about what it was  like for tarzan tim to run around tying  shoes and applying band-aids in a place  where no one looks like he did  i won't presume to speak for him  but i do know what it's like to be the  only across three years in management  consulting in 12 different projects  not once did i have a black or latinx  supervisor  team manager or managing partner  when i looked upwards craving a mentor  that looked like me  i struggled to find a business leader i  could connect with  and now i'm here at the gsb and i'm i'm  struck by this duality  on one hand we learn so much about the  benefits of diversity  research right here at stanford tells us  that diverse groups are 87 percent  better at making decisions  a recent study by the boston consulting  group says that diverse management teams  lead their companies to 19  higher revenues  better at making money better at making  decision and yet i still see so much  room for improvement  black and latinx individuals attend  college 10 to 20 percent less than their  white peers  even with a degree black college  graduates earn 21 percent less than  their white classmates  and this is exacerbated at the top  where racially diverse executives  executives of any non-white american  background  hold only 16 percent of total c-suite  positions  i want to see this change  creating and upholding diver spaces  matters to me  because i dream  of seeing people in color in positions  of power  feeling they belong  expanding access to summer camp can be a  big part of this change  i know because for me  camp made all the difference  camp allowed me to beat back that  gremlin in my head screeching you're an  imposter you don't belong  i remember back in middle school when i  was dealing with bullying  camp was the haven in which i could  prove to myself that i could be liked i  could make friends  i could respond to adversity  simply put camp and its leaders like  tarzan tim  showed me i belonged  and if i could go from outsider to  belonging at camp  i sure as hell could do it in the real  world  so  we know we do well by our children when  we send them to camp  and we know we do well by society when  we promote and uphold diverse spaces  how do we bring these two truths  together  i could deliver a whole other talk about  what society can do to diversify the  camp space  from directing tax dollars to camp  initiatives to increasing education for  minority parents about the benefits of  camp  and even yet i could deliver this whole  other talk about the camp industry could  do to diversify from targeting more  diverse camp directors to increasing  diversity equity inclusion training for  its staff  but i want to focus on the here and now  i want to focus on what you in this room  can do  to expand access to these crucially  important youth development programs  first  send your kids to camp and do so  thoughtfully  you may not have kids now but if and  when you do send them out there to play  in the woods make mud balls and stampede  through the wilderness  and if that's not their thing that's  okay  send them to basketball camp theater  camp science camp woodworking camp  imaginary skateboarding with a parrot on  your shoulder camp  the options are really endless  but choose that camp deliberately  select a camp that upholds a diverse  environment  vote with your wallet and demonstrate to  camp owners and directors across the  nation that providing an enriching  experience for children of all  backgrounds is a business imperative  and secondly  donate to scholarship programs  just about every camp organization has  its own campership program focused on  expanding opportunity of access  the truth is however many of these slots  go unapplied for because minority  families either incorrectly believe they  can afford camp or are afraid to send  their children away  certain non-profit organizations are  addressing this issue by directly  matching inner city families with  residential summer camp programs  via donations from individuals like you  and me  organizations such as the guardsmen camp  piles and project mori  pay full camp tuition so that financial  burdens don't bar children from these  life-changing programs  i'll confess  diversifying the camp space won't fix  all the achievement gaps i mentioned  earlier  even so  i can assert  with conviction  that a summer at camp can fundamentally  shift the life trajectory of a child  it can show them  they belong  it did for me  i started this talk by telling you about  tarzan tim and the power of an invested  adult  now  i'm asking you to be  that invested adult  thank you  [Music]

